            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.107,
            0.226,
            0.153
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.159,
                0.107,
                0.226,
                0.153
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.099,
            0.825,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In mathematics, statements or claims made without proof or verification are called ‘conjectures’. Anshu’s claim is a conjecture. We disproved this conjecture by finding a counterexample, i.e., an example where the conjecture is false."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.185,
            0.813,
            0.207
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finding the HCF of Numbers Using Prime Factorisation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.212,
            0.847,
            0.252
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We now see how to make use of the observations made so far to find common factors and the Highest Common Factor (HCF)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.258,
            0.148,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.113,
                0.258,
                0.148,
                0.284
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.261,
            0.775,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 1:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Find the common factors, and the HCF of 45 and 75."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.286,
            0.682,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Calculate the prime factorisation for both numbers:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.433,
            0.311,
            0.565,
            0.33
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "4 5 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.432,
            0.335,
            0.566,
            0.355
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "7 5 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 5 ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.361,
            0.299,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.38,
            0.846,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Factors of 45 are subparts of factors occurring in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 \\times 3 \\times 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and factors of 75 are subparts of factors occurring in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 \\times 5 \\times 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.419,
            0.846,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So the common factors should be subparts of both the factorisations. Can you write them down?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.48,
            0.225,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.159,
                0.48,
                0.225,
                0.523
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.489,
            0.824,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While exploring or solving problems you might also get some conjectures. You can try to reason and verify these and also share with the class."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.548,
            0.483,
            0.57
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "\\textcircled{3}  \\times \\overline { { 3 } } \\times 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.575,
            0.483,
            0.597
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_16.jpg",
          "content": "\\textcircled{3}  \\times 5 \\times 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.563,
            0.614,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.611,
            0.485,
            0.661
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_18.jpg",
          "content": "3 \\times 3 \\times \\textcircled { 5 }"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.626,
            0.613,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.674,
            0.5,
            0.697
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_20.jpg",
          "content": "3 \\times \\textcircled{3} \\times \\textcircled{5}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.563,
            0.689,
            0.615,
            0.708
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_21.jpg",
          "content": "3 \\times 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.701,
            0.489,
            0.724
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_22.jpg",
          "content": "\\textcircled{3} \\times \\textcircled{5} \\times 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.733,
            0.846,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3, 5, 3 × 5 = 15 are subparts of both, and hence, they are the common factors along with 1. The highest among them is 15. So, their HCF = 15."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.781,
            0.786,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 2: Find the common factors, and the HCF of 112 and 84."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.807,
            0.571,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Calculating prime factorisations, we get,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.831,
            0.657,
            0.852
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_26.jpg",
          "content": "1 1 2 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 7   \\mathrm { a n d }"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 27,
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.856,
            0.584,
            0.877
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_27.jpg",
          "content": "8 4 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 ."
        },
        {
